.TH VERIFY 8
.SH NAME
verify \- the self-tests that run on every boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
none
.SH DESCRIPTION
Vectra has no host-side test build.
Every layer proves itself on the machine that will run it, during boot,
and reports one line.
This page describes what a line looks like and how to read one that
failed.
The discipline behind the self-tests, and what a negative control is
for, belongs to whoever changes the kernel and is not this page's
business.
.SS Reading a line
A self-test's line begins with a subsystem's name and how many checks
it made, for example
.BR "disk 3 checks passed" .
A line that passed is tagged
.BR "[  ok  ]" .
A line that failed is tagged
.BR "[ FAIL ]"
and names the first check that failed, because later ones are almost
always its consequences.
Up to eight of the later failures follow as indented lines beginning
.BR "  also:" ,
tagged
.BR "[  ??  ]" ,
and a line saying how many more there were if the count ran past that.
.PP
Some numbers in a passing line move on every run and are not a
mistake: a round count under a fixed tick budget, a calibration, an
operation count.
They are measured rather than asserted, because what a self-test wants
is a timer that interrupts a thread partway through its work often
enough to find a race, and how much work happens between interrupts is
not the thing being proved.
.PP
One line is a control that reports what looks like a failure as a
pass.
The 9P payload check names how many readers a shared buffer spoiled,
on purpose: the check runs the same eight readers against a server
that answers out of one buffer for everybody, which is what a 9P
server looked like before request slots had one each, and the check is
that they corrupt each other.
A run where nothing was spoiled would be the check that failed.
.SS Tagged and untagged lines
A tagged line, in brackets, is the kernel's own log.
An untagged line is not status.
It is a self-test writing through the very path it is testing: a line
written to
.BR /dev/cons ,
a line a ring 3 program printed to its own descriptors, bytes that
went out over a wire.
.B tests/tools.rc
produces untagged lines this way, one
.B ok
or
.B FAIL
per tool it checks, each written by the shell script itself and not by
the kernel.
.SS tests/tools.rc
Every tool under
.B cmd/
is checked once by
.BR tests/tools.rc ,
run by
.IR rc (1)
as part of the boot.
Each check is one line of the script:
.IP
.EX
check name expected command-output...
.EE
.PP
.I Name
is the tool being checked,
.I expected
is the words its output should equal once joined by spaces, and the
rest of the line is the command whose output is compared.
A check that matches prints
.BI "ok " name
and counts toward the total.
One that does not prints
.BI "FAIL " name
and the words it got instead of the words it expected.
The script's exit word is the count and
.BR ok ,
for example
.BR "36 ok" ,
or
.B failed
followed by the name of every tool that did not match.
.PP
The kernel's own self-test starts the script and compares its exit
word against a constant,
.B TOOLS_OK
in
.BR kernel/user/verify.odin .
A check added to
.B tests/tools.rc
without updating that constant fails the boot's tools line, and names
the mismatched word, because the count is meant to be kept in step by
hand rather than trusted to agree with itself.
.SS The ring 3 test programs
Four programs, each an ordinary package under
.BR tests/ ,
are spawned by the kernel's self-test and checked by the single word
each exits with:
.BR ok ,
or the name of the first step that did not hold.
.B tests/abi
exercises the process ABI itself: arguments, the environment, the
heap.
.B tests/thread
exercises
.BR sys/libthread :
threads, channels,
.IR alt ,
locks, rendezvous.
.B tests/mui
lays out a tree of
.B sys/libmui
objects and checks the rectangles the toolkit computed, drawing
nothing.
.B tests/net
builds and reads back the wire formats in
.BR sys/libnet ,
with no card touched.
.PP
.B kernel/user/programs
is a different shape: one Odin package compiled once per name, so
each name yields a single-purpose flat image the kernel's own ring 0
harness runs directly, without a shell.
Thirty-one names, each one behavior: a program that never yields, one
that tries a privileged instruction, one that forks, one that maps
memory, one that catches a note.
Every one writes a mark before it does anything else, so the harness
can tell a program that never ran from one that ran and was refused.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B tests/tools.rc
the tool check script above
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/verify_payload.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/verify.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/programs
.br
.B tests/abi
.br
.B tests/thread
.br
.B tests/mui
.br
.B tests/net
.br
.B tests/tools.rc
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR boot (8),
.IR build (8),
.IR rc (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A boot that hangs rather than fails prints nothing further.
QEMU's
.B \-s
opens a
.I gdb
stub whether or not anything attaches, so a hung boot can be left
running and read afterward with
.BR "lldb build/vectra.elf -o 'gdb-remote localhost:1234'" .
